# Do Richer Countries Have Happier Citizens?  
Analyzing GDP, Minimum Wage, and Happiness in 2024

## Contributors
- Jeff Li  
- Elin Na 

## Summary
Many countries are commonly described as “wealthy” or “developed” because they have large gross domestic products (GDP) and relatively high wages. It is tempting to assume that people living in these countries are necessarily happier than people in poorer regions with lower incomes. However, these assumptions ignore important questions about how economic indicators relate to the lived experiences of citizens. In this project, we investigate whether richer countries with higher minimum wages are actually happier, and how economic factors compare to social and health-related indicators when explaining national happiness.

Our central research question is:

> **Does a country’s GDP and minimum wage determine the level of happiness for its citizens, and how do these factors compare to social and health indicators such as life expectancy, social support, and freedom?**

To answer this question, we combined two independent datasets from Kaggle. The first is the **World Happiness Report 2024** dataset, which reports each country’s average “happiness ladder score” along with explanatory variables such as social support, healthy life expectancy, freedom to make life choices, generosity, and perceptions of corruption. The second is a **Countries of the World 2023** dataset, which focuses on economic and structural indicators including nominal GDP, minimum wage, unemployment rate, labor force participation, tax measures, and demographic information.

We first profiled and cleaned each dataset separately, focusing on missing values, inconsistent country naming, and problematic records. We removed countries with substantial missing data and standardized formats (for example, converting GDP and minimum wage from formatted strings like `"$19,101,353,833"` to numeric values). We also identified and handled clear outliers, such as Afghanistan’s extremely low happiness score in the World Happiness dataset.

After cleaning, we performed a schema alignment step and integrated the datasets by country name. This produced an integrated dataset of **115 countries and 27 variables**, combining happiness metrics with economic, demographic, and health indicators. Because our research question specifically focuses on minimum wage, we excluded the 21 integrated countries that do not have statutory minimum wage laws (many of which are high-income countries relying on collective bargaining). Our final analysis dataset therefore contains **94 countries** with complete data on happiness, minimum wage, GDP, and our selected covariates.

On this combined dataset, we computed summary statistics, pairwise correlations, and linear regression models. We found that **social and health indicators are much more strongly associated with happiness than raw GDP levels**. In particular, social support, healthy life expectancy, freedom to make life choices, and minimum wage show robust positive correlations with happiness scores, whereas total GDP shows only a weak association. This suggests that simply being a “rich” country is not enough to guarantee high happiness; how resources are converted into social support, health, and personal freedom appears to matter more.

Our regression model, which includes GDP, minimum wage, life expectancy, social support, and freedom, explains over 80% of the variation in national happiness scores (R² ≈ 0.81). GDP itself is not a statistically significant predictor once we control for these other factors, while social support, freedom, minimum wage, and life expectancy remain significant. These results reinforce the idea that **the quality and structure of social and health systems, as well as labor protections, are more closely tied to happiness than aggregate wealth alone**.

Overall, our project demonstrates the value of integrating multiple data sources to answer a policy-relevant question. By combining a happiness survey dataset with an economic and demographic dataset, we can go beyond simple GDP rankings and highlight specific features—such as social support and life expectancy—that appear to contribute more directly to the wellbeing of citizens.

